   5. cite67†Centralized value guidance. L78:     10. cite68†A.10 Additional Theory L79:       1. cite69†Conditional paths and marginalization trick. L80:       2. cite70†Learning objective (DFM and conditional DFM). L81:   10. cite71†B Additional Experimental Results L82:     1. cite72†B.1 Varying the number of action divisions. L83:     2. cite73†B.2 Performance on discretized MuJoCo tasks L84:     3. cite74†B.3 Euler steps and rate-scale ablations L85:       1. cite75†CTMC simulation details and hyperparameters L86:       2. cite76†Stability of discrete sampling. L87:       3. cite77†Effect of the number of integration steps. L88:     4. cite78†B.4 CTMC Sampler Correctness in Intrinsically Discrete Control L89:       1. cite79†Setup. L90:       2. cite80†Baselines. L91:       3. cite81†CartPole. L92:       4. cite82†Acrobot. L93:     5. cite83†B.5 CartPole: Toy two-objective preference conditioning L94:     6. cite84†B.6 Multi-Objective Fork: Qualitative Evidence of Discrete Multimodality L95:     7. cite85†B.7 Preference-Conditioned Multi-objective Offline Reinforcement Learning on Resource-Gathering L96:       1. cite86†Environment. L97:       2. cite87†Offline data and training. L98:       3. cite88†Coverage of achievable behaviors. L99:       4. cite89†Response to preference changes. L100:     8. cite90†B.8 Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning: Matrix Game L101:       1. cite91†Offline dataset. L102:       2. cite92†Results. L103:   11. cite93†C Complete Training procedure L104: cite94†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L105: \nL106: arXiv:2602.06138v1 [cs.LG] 05 Feb 2026\nL107: # Flow Matching for Offline Reinforcement Learning\nL108: with Discrete Actions\nL109: Fairoz Nower Khan Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, University of Kentucky    Nabuat Zaman Nahim Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, University of Kentucky    Ruiquan Huang Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, University of Kentucky    Haibo Yang Affiliation: Department of Computing and Information Sciences, Rochester Institute of Technology    Peizhong Ju Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, University of Kentucky\nL110: ###### Abstract\nL111: Generative policies based on diffusion models and flow matching have shown strong promise for offline reinforcement learning (RL), but their applicability remains largely confined to continuous action spaces. To address a broader range of offline RL settings, we extend flow matching to a general framework that supports discrete action spaces with multiple objectives. Specifically, we replace continuous flows with continuous-time Markov chains, trained using a Q-weighted flow matching objective.\nL112: We then extend our design to multi-agent settings, mitigating the exponential growth of joint action spaces via a factorized conditional path. We theoretically show that, under idealized conditions, optimizing this objective recovers the optimal policy. Extensive experiments further demonstrate that our method performs robustly in practical scenarios, including high-dimensional control, multi-modal decision-making, and dynamically changing preferences over multiple objectives.\nL113: Our discrete framework can also be applied to continuous-control problems through action quantization, providing a flexible trade-off between representational complexity and performance.\nL114: ## 1 Introduction\nL115: Offline reinforcement learning (RL) has recently seen a shift toward generative policy modeling driven by the success of diffusion models and related continuous-time generative frameworks (cite95†Wang et al., 2022 ; cite96†Chen et al., 2023 ; cite97†Kang et al., 2023 ). By decoupling policy representation from direct maximization, generative approaches mitigate extrapolation error and enable principled value-based guidance.\nL116: Specifically, diffusion-based methods introduced value guidance either heuristically or via learned classifiers (cite98†Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ; cite99†Ho and Salimans, 2021 ). Subsequent work formalized this connection through energy-guided generative modeling, showing that diffusion scores can be interpreted as gradients of a KL-regularized optimal policy (cite100†Lu et al., 2023 ; cite101†Zheng et al., 2023 ).\nL117: As an important successor of diffusion models, flow matching models (cite102†Lipman et al., 2022 ; cite103†Liu, 2022 ; cite104†Lipman et al., 2024 ) have recently been applied to offline RL in cite105†Zhang et al. (2025) . By replacing stochastic diffusion dynamics with deterministic flow matching, cite105†Zhang et al. (2025) showed that energy guidance can be incorporated directly into the training objective via Q-weighted regression.\nL118: This eliminated the need for auxiliary classifiers and yielded efficient, theoretically grounded policy improvement in continuous action spaces.\nL119: However, applying flow matching models to RL is far from complete, since the existing generative offline RL methods are fundamentally restricted to continuous action spaces. Both diffusion models and flow matching rely on stochastic or deterministic differential equations defined on Euclidean domains (cite106†Chen et al., 2018 ; cite102†Lipman et al., 2022 ), making them ill-suited for discrete or combinatorial decision problems in RL.\nL120: In such settings, good decisions often lie in several distinct and incompatible modes, and forcing continuous generative models to interpolate between them can produce invalid actions and poor generalization when the dataset does not fully cover all behaviors (cite107†Yuan et al., 2025 ).\nL121: Meanwhile, applying generative offline RL to real-world domains such as recommendation, token-based generation, and structured control requires policy representations and training objectives that are native to discrete spaces. This raises a meaningful question:\nL122: How to adapt flow matching to RL with discrete actions?\nL123: The answer is not straightforward because the current restriction to continuous action spaces is not only a modeling choice but also a structural requirement of existing flow-based RL methods. Flow matching relies on ordinary differential equations (ODEs) that transport probability mass via a vector field defined on a Euclidean domain. RL policy improvement is achieved by coupling these dynamics with gradients of value or energy functions with respect to actions.\nL124: When the action space is discrete, however, this formulation breaks down: vector fields are undefined, action-space derivatives do not exist, and the continuity equation underlying flow matching no longer applies. The lack of essential ingredients, including action-gradient guidance and continuous change-of-variables arguments, prevents existing theoretical guarantees for continuous actions from carrying over directly.\nL125: As a result, extending energy-guided generative policy optimization to discrete offline RL requires rethinking the policy representation, training objective, and underlying theory.\nL126: #### Motivating Example.\nL127: Before introducing our solution, we illustrate why treating inherently discrete decisions with continuous generative models can lead to fundamental failures. Figure cite108†1 presents a simple multi-objective grid-world example in which the offline dataset contains two disjoint optimal strategies, while the intermediate region is a catastrophic trap state that is never observed.\nL128: This setting highlights a key limitation of continuous relaxations and regression-style policies: interpolating between discrete modes can produce invalid behavior that is absent from the data and leads directly to failure. This example motivates the need for a generative policy representation that can handle discrete spaces well.\nL129: cite109†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Motivating example a) Offline dataset exhibits two disjoint modes corresponding to different objectives (Goal A and Goal B); the central region is a trap state never observed in the data. b) A continuous treatment of the decision space can induce interpolation between the modes and potential collapse toward the mean, causing trajectories to enter the trap.\nL130: #### Our Method.\nL131: To answer the aforementioned question, we propose a new flow matching algorithm, Q-weighted discrete flow matching (QDFM), to solve the offline RL problem with a discrete action space. Our key idea is to replace continuous ODE-based RL policy representations with a Continuous-Time Markov Chain (CTMC) defined over a discrete action space, leveraging the recent Discrete Flow Matching (DFM) techniques (cite110†Campbell et al., 2024 ; cite111†Gat et al., 2024 ; cite104†Lipman et al., 2024 ).\nL132: Instead of learning a continuous vector field that transports probability mass via a continuity equation, our proposed algorithm targets a time-dependent rate function whose induced dynamics follow the Kolmogorov forward equation.\nL133: We then integrate DFM with value-based policy improvement by deriving a Q-weighted conditional objective that leads to an energy-weighted loss for discrete spaces. Theoretical analysis shows that in terms of the gradient of parameters, optimizing the weighted conditional DFM objective is equivalent to optimizing a guided marginal objective for the exact KL-regularized (Boltzmann) optimal policy.\nL134: This work establishes a theoretical guarantee for energy-weighted generative policies in discrete action spaces, showing that CTMC-based flows recover the KL-regularized optimal policy without auxiliary classifiers.\nL135: To address a wider range of practical scenarios, we develop our algorithm within a general multi-objective offline RL framework where the classical single-objective setting is a special case. By conditioning the flow matching objective on randomly sampled preference vectors during training, the resulting model can efficiently generate actions for changing preferences at inference time without retraining.\nL136: Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that our algorithm performs robustly across high-dimensional discretized environments, intrinsically discrete control tasks, multi-objective benchmarks and a multi-agent game highlighting the adaptability of our design. To the best of our knowledge, this work presents the first principled framework for energy-guided generative policy optimization in discrete action spaces, extended to preference-conditioned multi-objective settings with theoretical guarantees.\nL137: ## 2 Related Work\nL138: \nL139: There are several types of work related to this paper. We briefly introduce them as follows.\nL140: #### Heuristic and Classifier-based Diffusion for RL.\nL141: Diffusion-based methods were used to model policies as conditional generative processes trained via behavior cloning and guided toward high-value actions (cite95†Wang et al., 2022 ; cite96†Chen et al., 2023 ; cite97†Kang et al., 2023 ).\nL142: Early approaches relied on heuristic guidance or classifier-based correction during sampling (cite98†Dhariwal and Nichol, 2021 ; cite99†Ho and Salimans, 2021 ), which required additional models and training procedures where different components must be trained together in a coordinated, interdependent way which increased algorithmic complexity.\nL143: #### Energy-Guided Diffusion and Flow Matching for RL.\nL144: Contrastive Energy Prediction (CEP) formalized value guidance by learning a time-dependent energy model whose gradients steer diffusion sampling toward the KL-regularized optimal policy (cite100†Lu et al., 2023 ). Guided flows further generalized this idea, interpreting guidance as a modification of probability flows in continuous time (cite101†Zheng et al., 2023 ).\nL145: Energy-Weighted Flow Matching (cite105†Zhang et al., 2025 ) advanced this line of work by incorporating Q-based guidance directly into the flow matching objective, eliminating the need for auxiliary classifiers and providing theoretical guarantees for policy recovery in continuous action spaces . Our work builds directly on this insight but departs fundamentally in representation by moving from continuous ODE flows to discrete CTMC dynamics.\nL146: #### Discrete Flow Matching and CTMC.\nL147: Discrete Flow Matching extends the flow matching framework to discrete state spaces by replacing ODE dynamics with Continuous-Time Markov Chains (CTMCs) governed by the Kolmogorov forward equation (cite111†Gat et al., 2024 ; cite104†Lipman et al., 2024 ). This framework has been successfully applied to structured generation problems, including graphs and biological sequences (cite112†Qin et al., 2024 ; cite113†Chen et al., 2025 ).\nL148: Recent theoretical analyses have established learnability and consistency properties of discrete flow matching models (cite114†Su et al., 2025 ; cite115†Wan et al., 2025 ). However, prior work has not explored the integration of discrete flow matching with reinforcement learning objectives or value-based policy improvement.\nL149: #### Diffusion for Multi-Objective RL.\nL150: \nL151: Recent work cite107†Yuan et al. (2025) has explored multi-objective RL using diffusion-based generative policies in continuous action spaces, where a preference vector across objectives is used as the condition of the diffusion RL model. In this work, we use a similar approach of treating the preference vector as the condition, but for flow matching with discrete actions instead of diffusion with continuous actions.\nL152: \nL153: ## 3 Preliminaries\nL154: ### 3.1 Problem Setup and Notations\nL155: We consider a multi-objective Markov decision process (MOMDP) $\\mathcal{M}$ which consists of $(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{A},\\mathbf{r},P,\\gamma)$, where $\\mathcal{S}$ is the state space, $\\mathcal{A}$ is the action space, $\\mathbf{r}:\\mathcal{S}\\times\\mathcal{A}\\mapsto\\mathbb{R}^{K}$ is a vector-valued reward for $K$ objectives, $P$ is the transition kernel, and $\\gamma\\in(0,1)$ is the discount factor.\nL156: In this work, we focus on the situation of discrete actions (so the number of actions $\\left|A\\right|$ is finite). The state space can be either discrete or continuous.\nL157: The offline dataset $\\mathcal{D}$ consists of $N$ samples:\nL158: \nL159:  | $$\\mathcal{D}=\\{(s_{i},a_{i},\\mathbf{r}_{i},s^{\\prime}_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{N},$$  |\nL160: \nL161: where $s_{i}^{\\prime}$ denotes the state after taking an action $a_{i}$ on the state $s_{i}$, and $\\mathbf{r}_{i}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{K}$ denotes the reward vector.\nL162: In our multi-objective setting, the notion of a state–action value function depends explicitly on a user preference over objectives. We learn a vector-valued critic $\\mathbf{Q}_{\\psi}:\\mathcal{S}\\times\\mathcal{A}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{K},$ which predicts one state-action value per objective. For a given preference vector $\\omega\\in\\Delta^{K-1}$, we define the scalarized reward $r_{\\omega}(s,a):=\\langle\\omega,\\mathbf{r}(s,a)\\rangle$. The state–action value function (Q-function) is then defined as\nL163:  | $$Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\;:=\\;\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty}\\gamma^{t}r_{\\omega}(s_{t},a_{t})\\;\\middle|\\;s_{0}=s,\\;a_{0}=a\\;\\right]$$  |  | (1)\nL164: \nL165: In our implementation, $Q_{\\omega}(s,a)$ is represented by scalarizing the learned vector critic:\nL166: \nL167:  | $$Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\;=\\;\\langle\\omega,\\mathbf{Q}_{\\psi}(s,a)\\rangle.$$  |  | (2)\nL168: For each fixed state $s$ and preference vector $\\omega$, the optimal policy under KL-regularized policy optimization admits a closed-form solution. Specifically, when maximizing expected return while penalizing deviation from the behavior policy $\\mu(\\cdot\\mid s)$ via a Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence, the optimal policy is given by a Boltzmann distribution (cite116†Todorov, 2006 ; cite117†Haarnoja et al., 2017 ; cite100†Lu et al., 2023 ):\nL169:  | $$\\pi^{\\star}_{\\omega}(a\\mid s)=\\mu(a\\mid s)\\exp\\!\\big(\\beta Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big)/C(s),$$  |  | (3)\nL170: where $C(s)$ is the normalization factor^{1}^{1} 1 When the context is clear, we omit the normalization factor by writing $\\pi^{\\star}_{\\omega}(a|s)\\propto\\mu(a|s)\\exp\\!\\big(\\beta Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big)$. $C(s)=\\sum_{a}\\mu(a|s)\\exp\\!\\big(\\beta Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\big)$, and $\\beta>0$ is a temperature parameter that controls the strength of value-based guidance: larger $\\beta$ emphasizes more on high-value actions, while smaller $\\beta$ keeps the policy closer to the behavior policy.\nL171: ### 3.2 Flow Matching\nL172: Flow matching is a generative modeling framework that constructs a target distribution by learning a time-dependent velocity field that transports probability mass from a simple source distribution to a desired target distribution (cite102†Lipman et al., 2022 ; cite103†Liu, 2022 ). Given a family of intermediate distributions $\\{p_{t}\\}_{0\\leq t\\leq 1}$ connecting a source $p_{0}$ to a target $p_{1}$, flow matching learns a vector field $v_{t}(x)$ whose induced dynamics satisfy the continuity equation\nL173:  | $$\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}p_{t}(x)=-\\nabla_{x}\\cdot\\big(p_{t}(x)\\,v_{t}(x)\\big),$$  |  | (4)\nL174: \nL175: which describes the conservation of probability mass under deterministic transport.\nL176: \nL177: In practice, sampling is performed by solving the ordinary differential equation (ODE)\nL178: \nL179:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}X_{t}=v_{t}(X_{t}),$$  |  | (5)\nL180: which deterministically maps samples from $p_{0}$ to $p_{1}$. The velocity field $v_{t}$ is learned by matching it to a prescribed probability path, rather than by maximizing likelihood or simulating stochastic diffusion dynamics.\nL181: Recent work has shown that flow matching can be integrated with value-based guidance for offline reinforcement learning, yielding efficient policy improvement in continuous action spaces (cite105†Zhang et al., 2025 ). However, both the continuity Eq. (cite118†4 ) and the ODE Eq. (cite119†5 ) rely fundamentally on gradients with respect to the underlying space, which makes this formulation incompatible with discrete action spaces.\nL182: ### 3.3 Discrete Flow Matching\nL183: Discrete Flow Matching (DFM) extends flow matching from continuous Euclidean spaces to discrete spaces by modeling probability flows with a Continuous-Time Markov Chain (CTMC) (cite110†Campbell et al., 2024 ; cite104†Lipman et al., 2024 ). Where continuous flow matching learns a vector field governed by a continuity equation, DFM learns a rate field governed by the Kolmogorov forward equation. DFM provides a principled way to construct probability flows on discrete spaces using CTMC dynamics.\nL184: #### Kolmogorov forward equation.\nL185: \nL186: Let $p_{t}$ denote the marginal probability mass function (PMF) of $X_{t}\\in\\mathcal{X}$. The CTMC dynamics evolve according to the Kolmogorov forward equation:\nL187: \nL188:  | $$\\frac{d}{dt}p_{t}(y)=\\sum\\nolimits_{x\\in\\mathcal{X}}u_{t}(y,x)\\,p_{t}(x).$$  |  | (6)\nL189: #### CTMC rate field.\nL190: \nL191: Let $\\mathcal{X}$ be a finite discrete space. In this work, $\\mathcal{X}$ corresponds to the discrete action space $\\mathcal{A}$. A CTMC is specified by time-dependent transition rates $u_{t}(y,x)$ for $x,y\\in\\mathcal{X}$ (also called a generator or velocity field), satisfying the standard rate (generator) constraints:\nL192: \nL193:  | $$u_{t}(y,x)\\geq 0\\text{ for all }y\\neq x,\\text{ and }\\sum\\nolimits_{y}u_{t}(y,x)=0.$$  |  | (7)\nL194: In discrete spaces, the object that replaces a velocity field is a rate matrix; learning it lets us “flow” probability mass by jumps rather than derivatives.\nL195: ## 4 Method: Discrete Preference-Conditioned Flow Matching on Offline RL\nL196: \nL197: Our goal is to learn a flow matching model that can generate discrete actions following an optimized policy in an offline RL setting. To that end, we need to replace continuous ODE dynamics and action-space derivatives in the existing flow-based offline RL methods. Specifically, we consider a Markov jump process over discrete actions, and learn its transition rates from the corresponding flow.\nL198: ### 4.1 Proposed Algorithm\nL199: Algorithm cite120†1 sketched the structure of our algorithm at a high level. Phase 1 performs an unweighted generative warm-up, in which the rate network is trained via discrete flow matching to map a simple base distribution over actions to the empirical behavior distribution. This initialization is critical: it ensures that the learned CTMC places probability mass only on actions supported by the offline dataset, providing a well-calibrated generative model before value-based guidance is introduced.\nL200: Phase 2 learns a preference-conditioned vector critic from the offline dataset, which is used to guide policy improvement in the next phase. Finally, Phase 3 performs policy improvement by reweighting endpoint actions according to $Q_{\\omega}$ and refining the rate model via weighted conditional flow matching, biasing the generative dynamics toward higher-value actions while remaining within the data support.\nL201: In the following, we will explain Algorithm cite120†1 in detail in terms of: i) the validity of the flow, ii) the rationality of the training objective, and iii) the inference algorithm.\nL202: \nL203: Algorithm 1 Q-weighted discrete flow matching for offline RL (sketched version)\nL204: 1:  Input: Offline RL Dataset $\\mathcal{D}$, rate function $u_{\\theta,t}$, guidance scale $\\hat{\\beta}>0$, behavior policy $\\hat{\\mu}(\\cdot|s)$ (learned from standard offline RL behavior cloning), support action set size M, number of epochs $K_{1},K_{2}$ and $K_{3}$\nL205: \nL206: 2:  Phase 1: flow matching model warm-up\nL207: \nL208: 3:  for warm-up step $k=1,2,\\dots,K_{1}$ do\nL209: \nL210: 4:   Sample batch $(s,a)\\subset\\mathcal{D}$, preference $\\omega\\sim\\text{Uniform}$,\nL211: \nL212: 5:   Update $u_{\\theta,t}$ by minimizing the unweighted DFM loss\nL213: 6:  end for\nL214: \nL215: 7:  Phase 2: critic learning\nL216: \nL217: 8:  for critic learning step $k=1,2,\\dots,K_{2}$ do\nL218: \nL219: 9:   Sample batch $(s,a,\\mathbf{r},s^{\\prime})\\subset\\mathcal{D}$ and preference $\\omega\\sim\\text{Uniform}$.\nL220: \nL221: 10:   Scalarize: $Q_{\\omega}(s,a)\\leftarrow\\langle\\omega,\\mathbf{Q}_{\\psi}(s,a)\\rangle$.\nL222: \nL223: 11:   Update vector critic $\\mathbf{Q}_{\\psi}$ via Bellman regression on the scalarized values $Q_{\\omega}$.\nL224: \nL225: 12:  end for\nL226: \nL227: 13:  Phase 3: iterative policy improvement\nL228: 14:  for policy improvement step $k=1,2,\\dots,K_{3}$ do\nL229: \nL230: 15:   Sample batch $(s,a)$, preference $\\omega\\sim\\text{Uniform}$, and endpoints $\\{A_{1}^{(j)}\\}_{j=1}^{M}$ using $u_{\\theta,t}(\\cdot,\\cdot\\mid s,\\omega)$ (by running the CTMC inference in Algorithm cite121†2 )\nL231: \nL232: 16:   Compute guidance weights using the scalarized Q-value: $w_{j}\\propto\\exp\\!\\big(\\hat{\\beta}\\langle\\omega,\\mathbf{Q}_{\\psi}(s,A_{1}^{(j)})\\rangle\\big)$ (Eq. (cite122†11 )) over the sampled endpoints.\nL233: \nL234: 17:   Start $A_{0}\\leftarrow a$ (Dataset)\nL235: 18:   Update $u_{\\theta,t}$ by minimizing the Q-weighted conditional DFM loss (Eq. (cite123†10 )) with weights $\\{w_{j}\\}$, biasing the flow toward high-value endpoints.\nL236: \nL237: 19:  end for\nL238: \nL239: 20:  Output: Trained rate function $u_{\\theta,t}$\nL240: ### 4.2 Discrete Flow and its Validity in Offline RL\nL241: We first introduce the necessary notations. We use $A_{t}$ for the (discrete-action-valued) random variable of the flow indexed by continuous time $0\\leq t\\leq 1$. A conditional path is conditioned on $Z\\coloneqq(s,\\omega,A_{0},A_{1})$, where $A_{0}$ is the initialization action and $A_{1}$ is the endpoint action. The behavior distribution that generates the offline dataset is $\\mu(\\cdot\\mid s)$, while $\\hat{\\mu}$ denotes the estimated (learned) version of $\\mu$.\nL242: We use $\\delta(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ for the Kronecker delta (a function that outputs $1$ when two inputs are the same, while outputs $0$ otherwise), and $\\gamma\\in(0,1)$ for the discount factor in RL.\nL243: The conditional flow path we use is\nL244: \nL245:  | $$p_{t\\mid Z}(a)=(1-t)\\delta(a,A_{0})+t\\delta(a,A_{1}),\\ 0\\leq t\\leq 1.$$  |  | (8)\nL246: \nL247: The target of Algorithm cite120†1 is learning a time-dependent rate function\nL248: \nL249:  | $\\displaystyle u_{\\theta,t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega).$  |\nL250: In order to make sure that the CTMC implied by this rate function is valid, the rate function $u_{\\theta,t}$ needs to satisfy the rate constraints $u_{\\theta,t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega)\\geq 0\\text{ for all }a^{\\prime}\\neq a$ and $\\sum_{a^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{A}}u_{\\theta,t}(a^{\\prime},a\\mid s,\\omega)=0$.\nL251: #### Source and endpoint distributions.\nL252: The conditional path in Eq. (cite124†8 ) is specified by a start action $A_{0}$ and an endpoint action $A_{1}$, whose roles depend on the training phase. During the generative warm-up (Phase 1), we sample $A_{0}\\sim\\mathrm{Uniform}$ and draw the endpoint $A_{1}\\sim\\hat{\\mu}(\\cdot\\mid s)$, encouraging the model to map a base distribution to the dataset-supported action distribution. During policy improvement (Phase 3), we instead set $A_{0}\\leftarrow a$ for $(s,a)\\sim\\mathcal{D}$.\nL253: For the target, we generate a support set $\\{A_{1}^{(j)}\\}_{j=1}^{M}$ of size $M$ by simulating the current rate model $u_{\\theta,t}$ (using Algorithm cite121†2 ). These generated candidates are then reweighted according to $Q_{\\omega}$, effectively biasing the probability flow toward the highest-value regions of the model’s current distribution.\nL254: #### A valid conditional CTMC generator.\nL255: To train the rate model $u_{\\theta,t}$ via conditional discrete flow matching, we require a ground-truth conditional rate field $u_{t}(\\cdot,\\cdot\\mid Z)$ against which the learned rate is compared in the flow-matching objective given in Eq. (cite123†10 ). This target rate must (i) define a valid Continuous-Time Markov Chain (CTMC), and (ii) induce the desired conditional interpolation path $p_{t\\mid Z}$ in Eq. (cite124†8 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlow Matching for Offline Reinforcement Learning with Discrete Actions (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.06138v1)\nciteturn27092view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.06138v1\",\"pattern\":\"Experiment\"}); Total lines: 1057\nNo matching text found for \"Experiment\""
